WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:08.120
I really enjoyed working for [Athletic Director] Jim Corbett because he was intellectually stimulating, you know.

2
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.860
“Hey, Fighting Tigers!” the fight song, do you know that story?

3
00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:12.860
No.

4
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:14.860
Okay.

5
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.440
He goes to New York on TV business.

6
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:24.820
See, he was on the NCAA Television Committee, a very important role in those days.

7
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:30.240
There was a play on Broadway called Wildcat.

8
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.600
Lucille Ball had the lead role.

9
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.340
There was a very lively tune called, “Hey!

10
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.340
Look Me Over.”

11
00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.400
To Jim Corbett, that sounded like a college fight song.

12
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.160
He turned “Hey!

13
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:48.060
Look Me Over” into “Hey, Fighting Tigers” for [Coach Charles] McClendon’s first game in Tiger Stadium.

14
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:53.280
Got somebody on campus to write the words, got permission from the people to use it.

15
00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:59.580
Tom Tyra, the band director . . . I was in this staff meeting and Tom Tyra said, “It’ll always be known as, ‘Hey!

16
00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:00.060
Look Me Over.’”

17
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:06.580
Now, you find me somebody in Tiger Stadium today that knows that that was, “Hey!

18
00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:07.780
Look Me Over.”

19
00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:17.620
That’s not your normal athletic director that didn’t have any journalism background, that didn’t have any creativity about him.

20
00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:23.980
You know, they were an old baseball coach or an old football coach and you didn’t have . . . you know.

21
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.980
If you saw an offense you liked you copied it, you know.

22
00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.170
You didn’t have to be creative.
